---
/** /design, the type: the real classes on specimen words, and the ticking figures. */
import { tools } from "../../lib/tools";

/** A real tool name, so the entry-title specimen is set in a word that ships. */
const entryTitle = tools[0]?.name ?? "Tools";
---

<section>
  <h2 class="sheet__head mono">Type</h2>
  <p>
    Geist Sans for prose and labels, Geist Mono for data: dates, counts,
    clocks, hex values and code. Both are variable fonts served from this
    domain.
  </p>

  {/*
    Every specimen is a `div` and not a `p`, which is the difference between
    showing the class and showing this page's prose rule. `.sheet p` sets a
    reading size, and Astro scopes it with the page's own class, so it
    outranks `.page-title` and every specimen would have come out at 1rem
    wearing the right letter-spacing. A specimen of a heading treatment is
    not a paragraph anyway.
  */}
  <dl class="spec">
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">page-title</dt>
      <dd><div class="page-title">Aa</div></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">page-title--entry</dt>
      <dd><div class="page-title page-title--entry">{entryTitle}</div></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">page-title--mono</dt>
      <dd><div class="page-title page-title--mono">design.</div></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">standfirst</dt>
      <dd>
        <div class="standfirst">One line under every title, and never two.</div>
      </dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">mono</dt>
      <dd><div class="mono spec__label">Cliff notes</div></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">mono + time</dt>
      <dd>
        <div class="mono spec__label">Saved <time datetime="2026-09-22">Sep 22, 2026</time></div>
      </dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <p>
    The masthead at the top of this page is the mono variant and the line
    under it is the standfirst, so two of those rows are already in front of
    you at full size, in the elements they normally ship as. The class
    called <span class="code">mono</span> is the label voice, and it is not
    mono any more: 14px sans, weight 400, normal tracking, sentence case,
    for section heads, field labels, tabs and footer items. The titles are
    bold rather than big, and everything under them stays quiet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two rules are global and are not in the table because nothing sets them:
    every heading balances its line breaks and every paragraph gets the
    prettier ones. Both are declared once, for the elements, and no component
    redeclares them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mono is for what you might retype: a count, a clock, a hex, a
    key cap like <span class="code">esc</span>, a token name. Inside a label
    the figure switches to mono by itself, because every figure already
    carries tabular numbers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Text comes in four levels, and each piece of text is one of them. The
    fourth never carries a word; it draws the slash between crumbs.
  </p>

  <dl class="spec">
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">--text-primary</dt>
      <dd><div class="level level--fg">Titles, names, links</div></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">--text-secondary</dt>
      <dd><div class="level level--muted">Prose, notes, and every control</div></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">--text-tertiary</dt>
      <dd><div class="level level--faint">Labels, dates, counts</div></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">--text-quaternary</dt>
      <dd><div class="level level--quaternary" aria-hidden="true">/</div></dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <p>
    Spacing runs on three numbers: 8px inside a group, 16px between groups,
    40px between sections. On an entry page the title, its metadata and its
    one-line summary sit 16px apart, and the notes under them start a new
    section.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tabular numbers are not optional. Any digit that changes in place, or
    sits in a column, gets them. Here is the same clock twice, the second one
    without.
  </p>

  <dl class="spec">
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">tabular-nums</dt>
      <dd><span class="figures tabular-nums" data-tick>--:--:--</span></dd>
    </div>
    <div class="spec__row">
      <dt class="code">default</dt>
      <dd><span class="figures" data-tick>--:--:--</span></dd>
    </div>
  </dl>

  <p>
    Watch the right edge of the second one. Every time a digit changes width
    the whole string re-lays-out, which is a twitch in the corner of your eye
    on a page with forty dated rows on it.
  </p>
</section>

<style>

  /* The four text levels, each at the size it most often ships at. */
  .level--fg {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }


  .level--muted {
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }


  .level--faint {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
  }


  .level--quaternary {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-quaternary);
  }


  /* --- type ------------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* The figures specimen is set in the sans at reading size, because Geist Mono
     is monospaced and would make the comparison meaningless: both rows would
     hold still and the point would be invisible. This is where the site actually
     spends the feature, on sans digits in a column. */
  .figures {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }
</style>
